WEBVTT

00:00:00.381 --> 00:00:02.645
Last time, we saw that losing the outer

00:00:02.685 --> 00:00:05.110
electron gets easier as you go down Group

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:05.350
one.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.660
Now let's see what that looks like.

00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:11.613
Lithium, sodium and potassium are all Group one

00:00:11.655 --> 00:00:13.640
metals, called the alkali metals.

00:00:14.303 --> 00:00:17.013
Each atom has one outer electron, and each

00:00:17.115 --> 00:00:19.142
metal makes the same products with water.

00:00:19.906 --> 00:00:22.189
But drop them in, and lithium just fizzes

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.874
calmly, while potassium bursts into flame.

00:00:25.696 --> 00:00:27.561
So why do Group one metals react so

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:28.664
differently with water?

00:00:29.673 --> 00:00:30.584
First, safety.

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:32.837
You won't handle these metals yourself.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.088
They react with the moisture on your skin,

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.640
so they're stored under oil and never touched.

00:00:38.284 --> 00:00:40.634
In school, these reactions are shown only as

00:00:40.676 --> 00:00:42.785
a teacher demonstration, or on video.

00:00:43.349 --> 00:00:45.143
So here, we'll watch them as short video

00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:45.405
clips.

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:48.878
Rubidium and caesium, further down the group, are

00:00:48.999 --> 00:00:50.423
never used in schools at all.

00:00:51.192 --> 00:00:51.924
Here's lithium.

00:00:52.548 --> 00:00:54.650
It's the least dense metal of all, so

00:00:54.711 --> 00:00:56.592
it floats, sitting high on the water,

00:00:57.154 --> 00:00:59.155
and it fizzes steadily as a gas is

00:00:59.176 --> 00:00:59.575
given off.

00:01:00.358 --> 00:01:02.515
It slowly moves around and gets smaller.

00:01:03.161 --> 00:01:03.786
It doesn't melt.

00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:06.057
A few drops of indicator in the water

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.024
slowly turn pink around it.

00:01:08.772 --> 00:01:09.543
Now sodium.

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:12.559
It floats too, but the reaction is faster.

00:01:13.122 --> 00:01:15.352
It melts into a shiny silver ball that

00:01:15.514 --> 00:01:17.644
races around the surface, fizzing hard.

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:20.315
It melts because the reaction gives out heat,

00:01:20.678 --> 00:01:22.886
and sodium melts at only ninety-eight degrees.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.676
If it's held still, it can even catch

00:01:25.797 --> 00:01:27.903
fire, with a yellow-orange flame.

00:01:28.446 --> 00:01:30.142
The pink spreads much more quickly.

00:01:30.952 --> 00:01:31.983
And potassium.

00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:35.474
It melts at only sixty-three degrees, so it

00:01:35.596 --> 00:01:36.476
melts at once,

00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:39.342
and the gas it gives off catches fire

00:01:39.423 --> 00:01:42.108
straight away, burning with a lilac flame.

00:01:42.792 --> 00:01:44.910
It crackles, and it may spit.

00:01:45.575 --> 00:01:47.280
This is the most vigorous of the three

00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:48.605
we can show in a school.

00:01:49.296 --> 00:01:50.062
Here's the surprise.

00:01:50.648 --> 00:01:52.603
All three make the same two products.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:54.496
The gas is hydrogen.

00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:57.419
The water becomes a solution of the metal

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.101
hydroxide, which is alkaline, and that's why the

00:02:00.202 --> 00:02:01.262
indicator turns pink.

00:02:02.064 --> 00:02:02.565
For sodium,

00:02:03.068 --> 00:02:07.245
sodium plus water gives sodium hydroxide plus hydrogen.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.939
Lithium and potassium do exactly the same, each

00:02:10.981 --> 00:02:12.867
giving a fizz of hydrogen and a pink

00:02:12.888 --> 00:02:13.308
solution.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:15.469
So the difference isn't what's made.

00:02:15.854 --> 00:02:16.667
It's how fast.

00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:18.632
Why the different speeds?

00:02:19.477 --> 00:02:22.346
In each reaction, every metal atom loses its

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.457
one outer electron, and becomes a positive ion.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.984
The water takes that electron, which is what

00:02:28.026 --> 00:02:29.709
makes the hydrogen and the hydroxide.

00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:32.512
Last time, we saw that going down the

00:02:32.553 --> 00:02:34.913
group, the outer electron is further from the

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.635
nucleus, with the same core charge, so it's

00:02:37.676 --> 00:02:38.696
held less tightly.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:42.190
Potassium gives up its electron most readily, so

00:02:42.311 --> 00:02:43.837
its reaction runs fastest.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.724
A faster reaction also heats the metal faster,

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.290
so sodium and potassium melt, and potassium's hydrogen

00:02:50.452 --> 00:02:51.112
catches fire.

00:02:51.957 --> 00:02:52.521
Quick prediction.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:56.280
Rubidium comes next in Group one, below potassium.

00:02:56.985 --> 00:02:58.440
What would happen if it touched water?

00:02:59.108 --> 00:03:00.937
Think about where its outer electron sits.

00:03:01.664 --> 00:03:02.008
Have a think.

00:03:05.797 --> 00:03:06.643
Even more violent.

00:03:07.066 --> 00:03:09.992
Rubidium's outer electron is even further out, and

00:03:10.053 --> 00:03:12.358
its first ionisation energy is lower again.

00:03:13.121 --> 00:03:16.564
Rubidium and caesium react explosively with water, which

00:03:16.605 --> 00:03:17.946
is why we only see them in video

00:03:17.987 --> 00:03:18.307
footage.

00:03:19.051 --> 00:03:22.029
Caesium, below rubidium, is more violent still.

00:03:22.433 --> 00:03:24.010
The trend holds all the way down.

00:03:24.581 --> 00:03:27.190
When you compare these reactions, the conditions must

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:27.591
match.

00:03:28.313 --> 00:03:30.360
A bigger piece has more surface touching the

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.166
water, so it reacts faster.

00:03:32.829 --> 00:03:34.518
Warmer water speeds things up too.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.042
So a fair comparison uses the same size

00:03:38.082 --> 00:03:40.284
of piece, the same amount of water at

00:03:40.304 --> 00:03:41.044
the same temperature,

00:03:41.645 --> 00:03:43.066
and a freshly cut surface.

00:03:43.748 --> 00:03:46.074
Change those, and you change what you see,

00:03:46.416 --> 00:03:47.418
without changing the metal.

00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:49.356
That last condition matters.

00:03:50.058 --> 00:03:52.819
Alkali metals react with oxygen and water vapour

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.561
in the air, so a dull film, called

00:03:55.662 --> 00:03:57.463
tarnish, forms on the surface.

00:03:58.247 --> 00:04:00.079
That's another reason they're kept under oil.

00:04:00.946 --> 00:04:03.677
Cut sodium, and the fresh face is shiny

00:04:03.718 --> 00:04:06.027
silver, but it goes dull within minutes.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.039
A thick film separates the metal from the

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.589
water at first, so the reaction can start

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:12.172
more slowly.

00:04:12.936 --> 00:04:15.384
A slow start doesn't mean a less reactive

00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:15.685
metal.

00:04:16.338 --> 00:04:17.573
So here's the chain to keep.

00:04:18.078 --> 00:04:20.806
The further out the outer electron, the easier

00:04:20.847 --> 00:04:21.368
it comes off,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.555
and the faster the metal reacts.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:24.841
Further out,

00:04:25.425 --> 00:04:25.966
easier off,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.253
faster fizz.

00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:30.042
Your turn.

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:34.132
In a teacher demonstration, equal-sized pieces of lithium

00:04:34.254 --> 00:04:36.375
and potassium are added to water.

00:04:36.978 --> 00:04:39.148
Potassium reacts much more vigorously.

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:42.183
Explain this difference in terms of atomic structure.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:43.541
Pause here to answer.

00:04:54.101 --> 00:04:56.813
Both atoms react by losing one outer electron.

00:04:57.254 --> 00:05:00.620
Potassium has two more occupied shells than lithium,

00:05:01.121 --> 00:05:03.105
so its outer electron is further from the

00:05:03.145 --> 00:05:03.586
nucleus,

00:05:04.227 --> 00:05:05.349
with the same core charge.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:06.994
It's held less tightly,

00:05:07.515 --> 00:05:10.162
so potassium loses it more readily, and reacts

00:05:10.223 --> 00:05:10.624
faster.

00:05:11.448 --> 00:05:13.942
Saying potassium has more electrons to give away

00:05:14.103 --> 00:05:14.626
is the slip.

00:05:15.149 --> 00:05:16.738
Each atom gives up just one.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:18.928
Last one.

00:05:19.572 --> 00:05:22.140
Two equal pieces of sodium are added to

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.725
water at the same temperature.

00:05:24.409 --> 00:05:26.138
Piece A was freshly cut.

00:05:26.722 --> 00:05:29.036
Piece B had been exposed to air, and

00:05:29.076 --> 00:05:30.142
its surface is dull.

00:05:30.924 --> 00:05:34.487
Predict which piece starts reacting faster, and explain.

00:05:35.471 --> 00:05:36.297
Pause here to answer.

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:47.372
Piece A.

00:05:48.098 --> 00:05:50.743
Piece B is coated with tarnish, a film

00:05:50.845 --> 00:05:53.129
formed by reacting with the air, which keeps

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.833
water away from the metal at first.

00:05:55.516 --> 00:05:58.291
Piece A's fresh metal touches the water straight

00:05:58.332 --> 00:05:58.573
away.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.705
Saying they react at the same rate, because

00:06:01.786 --> 00:06:03.551
they're the same element, is the trap.

00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:04.877
Same metal,

00:06:05.401 --> 00:06:06.104
different surface.

00:06:07.893 --> 00:06:08.560
One last clue.

00:06:09.108 --> 00:06:10.732
Potassium's flame was lilac,

00:06:11.275 --> 00:06:12.799
and sodium's was yellow-orange.

00:06:13.723 --> 00:06:15.914
Hydrogen burning on its own has a pale,

00:06:16.116 --> 00:06:17.382
almost invisible flame.

00:06:18.087 --> 00:06:19.916
So the colour must come from the metal.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.769
You'll see the same colours whenever these metals'

00:06:22.871 --> 00:06:24.658
compounds get into a Bunsen flame.

00:06:25.502 --> 00:06:27.163
Next time, we'll see what flame colours can

00:06:27.244 --> 00:06:27.523
show,

00:06:28.205 --> 00:06:30.105
and what they can't identify reliably.

00:06:33.095 --> 00:06:33.917
Here are the key notes.

00:06:34.441 --> 00:06:35.988
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:36.029 --> 00:06:36.450
to copy them.
